    "summary": "Epic Dark Star > Eyes of the World > China Doll suite, soaring Row Jimmy",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a marathon Dark Star → Eyes of the World → China Doll sequence and an inspired take on Row Jimmy. Jerry’s gritty guitar tone and lazy vocal phrasing lock in with Phil’s deep‑groove bass and Bill’s crisp drumming. Loose Lucy, Box of Rain and Here Comes Sunshine also stand out.",
    "review": "On February 15, 1973 at the Dane County Coliseum, the Dead delivered one of the most memorable performances of that year. The first set rolls through tight, energetic numbers—Loose Lucy, a bright Box of Rain and a soulful Row Jimmy—that set up a second‑set masterpiece: an expansive Dark Star that morphs seamlessly into Eyes of the World before resolving in a soaring China Doll. Jerry’s guitar is both gritty and lyrical, his “lazy” vocal delivery adding a country‑tinged flavor to the jam, while Phil carves out a massive bass solo that bridges Dark Star and Eyes of the World. Keith’s piano punctuates the transitions with inventive leads on China Cat Sunflower, and Bill Kreutzmann’s perfectly tuned toms and bright cymbal bells drive the rhythm section forward. The set closes with classic crowd‑pleasers—Here Comes Sunshine, Sugaree, Sugar Magnolia—and a jubilant encore of Uncle John’s Band and One More Saturday Night. Across all available recordings the performance feels relaxed yet focused, making this show a top‑tier example of early‑’73 improvisational chemistry.",
